          "X x",
          "Y y"
        ],
        [
          "Z z"
        ]
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "text": "2. The whole alphabet"
    },
    {
      "type": "example",
      "en": "A B C D E F G H I J K L M",
      "note": "first half"
    },
    {
      "type": "example",
      "en": "N O P Q R S T U V W X Y Z",
      "note": "second half"
    },
    {
      "type": "rule",
      "title": "The five vowels",
      "points": [
        "a, e, i, o, u",
        "All the other 21 letters are consonants.",
        "Y is sometimes like a vowel: my, happy."
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "text": "3. Grammar focus: alphabetical order"
    },
    {
      "type": "rule",
      "title": "Alphabetical order (A–Z)",
      "points": [
        "Dictionaries and name lists use alphabetical order.",
        "Look at the first letter: apple, book, cat.",
        "If the first letters are the same, look at the second letter: bag, bed, bus."
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "text": "Be careful!"
    },
    {
      "type": "mistake",
      "wrong": "Q is alone: qeen",
      "right": "queen",
      "why": "In English, Q is almost always followed by u: queen, question."
    },
    {
      "type": "tip",
      "text": "Practise: say the whole alphabet A to Z without stopping."
    }
  ],
